---
title: "The live preview loop"
description: "What survives a save (scroll position, kernel state, a running canvas) and how Ctrl-click jumps from the page back to the line that made it."
---

The reason Taliesin exists is the edit loop: change the source, and the preview
reflects it *immediately*, without reloading the page, losing your scroll, or
resetting any live content already on screen.

"Immediately" is the one word in this chapter worth checking rather than believing.
@sec-speed gives it in absolute milliseconds on one machine, with no comparison ratio
attached.

## Incremental updates

On every save the server re-parses the document, diffs the new block list against
the old one by content-hash id, and pushes **only the blocks that changed** over a
websocket. An unchanged block is never touched, so:

- your scroll position is preserved,
- a running `{js}` cell or Three.js canvas keeps its state,
- the page never flashes white.

The block you just edited **pulses** briefly when it re-renders, so your eye (or
your phone) lands on the change.

## Click-to-source and editor sync

In the browser preview, a plain click browses normally; **Ctrl-click (Cmd-click
on Mac) any block to open its exact source line in your editor** (including across
`{{< include >}}`d files) via a `vscode://` deep link. The client also speaks a
`tali-goto` / `tali-cursor` `postMessage` protocol, so an editor can host the preview and
add cursor sync without forking anything. The in-repo **VS Code companion**
(`editor/vscode/`) is exactly that host.

**A chapter of a book previews as the book.** Open the preview on any page that lives
under a `_site.yml` and the companion serves the whole project and opens it at that
page's URL, so you get the nav, the breadcrumb and working cross-page links instead of an
orphan page. One server serves every chapter: opening a second one reveals the preview you
already have, moved to that chapter. Moving your cursor into another chapter's file moves
the preview to it too, and Ctrl-clicking a block resolves against the page you are looking
at, not the one you opened. Nothing to configure — whether a document is in a project is a
fact about the directory it sits in.

The two directions have names, and they behave differently on purpose. **Inverse search**
(preview → editor) is the Ctrl-click above. **Forward search** (editor → preview) is split:
moving your cursor *marks* the matching block in the preview and deliberately does **not**
move the page, so scrolling somewhere to compare two figures survives your next keystroke.
When you do want the preview to come to you, press **`Ctrl+Alt+J`**
(**Taliesin: Reveal Cursor in Preview**), which scrolls it there and leaves the keyboard
focus in your editor.

::: {.callout-tip}
## Reuse the editor tab instead of opening a new one

The `vscode://` deep link routes through VS Code's normal "open in the active editor
group" logic, so by default a second Ctrl-click can land the same file in a *new* tab
rather than moving the cursor in the tab you already have open. VS Code offers no URL
flag to change this, but one editor setting fixes it: add
`"workbench.editor.revealIfOpen": true` to your `settings.json` and repeated
Ctrl-clicks reveal the existing tab and jump the cursor instead of duplicating it. (The
VS Code companion opens through the editor API and reuses the tab without this
setting.)
:::

## What the editor does while you write

The companion is a thin client over `taliesin lsp`, so everything below is answered by the
same binary that builds the document and enforced by the same vocabulary `check` uses. A
completion cannot suggest something the validator would reject.

- **Completion** for front-matter keys and values, cell options and their values, cell
  languages, div classes, cross-references, citations, shortcode names, anchor ids, math
  commands, and file paths anywhere a path is legal.
- **Div attributes, narrowed to the class you wrote.** `::: {.callout-note ` offers
  `title=`, `collapse=`, `icon=` and `appearance=`; a `.step` offers `lines=` and `state=`.
  A `.lemma` is *not* offered `collapse=`, because only `proof` has a collapse branch and
  suggesting it elsewhere would be recommending something that renders no differently.
- **Format Document** tidies pipe tables (columns padded to their widest cell, alignment
  markers preserved) and removes whitespace that renders as nothing: trailing spaces that
  are not a hard line break, runs of three or more blank lines, a missing or doubled final
  newline. **Your prose is never re-wrapped**, no line is re-indented, no emphasis marker is
  normalized: the rule is that formatting cannot change one byte of the rendered page, which
  is checked against every document in this repository on every build. A table with a row
  longer than its header is left exactly as written, because tidying it would mean deleting
  your text, and anything inside a code fence or an indented code block is left alone
  because there a trailing space may be yours on purpose.
- **Lists and blockquotes continue on Enter**, keeping the marker you chose (`-`, `*`, `+`,
  `>`, and task items). Pressing Enter on an empty item is how you leave the list.
- **Insert Math Symbol** (`Ctrl+Alt+M`) searches the math vocabulary by name, glyph or
  category, for the symbols you cannot spell well enough to complete.
- **`_site.yml` is validated** against the bundled JSON Schema, if you have the Red Hat YAML
  extension installed. Outside VS Code, `taliesin schema --out <dir>` writes the schema and
  tells you the one comment line to add.

### Restructuring without retyping

Four commands move whole sections around, as edits to the `.tmd` **buffer**. Put the cursor
anywhere in a section and:

| Command | Key | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| Move Section Up | `Ctrl+Alt+U` | swaps the section with the one before it |
| Move Section Down | `Ctrl+Alt+D` | swaps it with the one after it |
| Promote Heading | `Ctrl+Alt+[` | `###` becomes `##`, and everything nested under it rises too |
| Demote Heading | `Ctrl+Alt+]` | `##` becomes `###`, with its subtree |

A "section" is the heading your cursor is in plus everything down to the next heading of the
same or shallower level, so a move takes the subsections with it. The cursor rides along, on
the same line of the same section, which is what lets you press the key twice to move
something two places.

They move **siblings only**. A `###` that is the last subsection of its `##` will not step
down past that boundary, because doing so would quietly change which chapter it belongs to;
you get a message naming the two sections instead. Promote refuses at `#` and demote refuses
at `######` for the same reason: those are Markdown's ends, not ours.

**In a deck this is slide reordering.** A slide *is* a `##` section, so Move Section Down
moves the slide later in the deck, with its content. That is deliberate: the drag-to-reorder
gesture the preview once had was removed for making the preview a second editing surface,
and this is the same operation done in the place that owns the file.

### What a reference resolves to, without leaving the line

Four of these answer a question you would otherwise have to scroll to answer. None of
them needs a preview window open, and none is specific to VS Code — they are the
protocol, so `cmd = { "taliesin", "lsp" }` gets them in Neovim, Helix or Zed too.

- **Inlay hints** put the *resolved* value in dim text just past the thing that resolves,
  so `@fig-scree` reads `@fig-scree ⟨1⟩`, `[@bishop2006]` reads `[@bishop2006] ⟨Bishop
  2006⟩`, and an `{{< include >}}` reads `⟨42 lines⟩`. This is the answer to "which figure
  *is* that, and did I mean the other one" without jumping. A reference whose anchor lives
  in **another chapter** is perfectly valid but has no number on this page, and it gets
  **no hint at all** rather than a placeholder: a missing hint reads as "no information",
  where `⟨elsewhere⟩` would read as a claim.
- **Folding** follows the document's own structure instead of indentation. Front matter,
  each heading down to the next of equal or shallower level, `:::` divs and code fences
  each fold as a unit. Without it an editor folds `.tmd` by *indentation*, which means
  nothing in a format whose nesting is heading level and fences: collapsing a chapter to
  its headings is the point.
- **Highlight** marks the anchor under your cursor everywhere it occurs, its definition as
  a write and every reference as a read, so putting the caret on `fig-scree` shows you
  every place that figure is cited.
- **Expand selection** (`Alt+Shift+→` in VS Code) grows by structure rather than by the
  editor's word heuristics: word, then the enclosing inline construct, then the paragraph,
  then the `:::` div, then the whole section. Shrink with `Alt+Shift+←`.

::: {.callout-note}
## Inlay hints are on unless you turn them off

`editor.inlayHints.enabled` defaults to `on` in VS Code, so the `⟨…⟩` annotations appear
without opting in. If you want them only when you ask, set it to `offUnlessPressed`, which
shows them while `Ctrl+Alt` is held.
:::

### The rest of the book, from inside one chapter

A book is one project, but an editor shows you one file. These four close that gap. Like
the ones above, the first two are the protocol rather than the extension, so any LSP editor
gets them.

- **Go to definition crosses files.** `F12` on `@sec-priors` jumps to the chapter that
  defines it, not just to a definition in the file you are in. Your **open buffer still
  wins**: an anchor you just typed and have not saved resolves to where you typed it, never
  to the older copy on disk. Hovering a cross-chapter reference names the page that defines
  it, since the *number* belongs to that page and is not known here.
- **Go to symbol in workspace** (`Ctrl+T`) reaches every heading and every anchor in the
  project, so you can jump to a section six chapters away without first remembering which
  file it lives in.
- **The Taliesin sidebar** adds three read-only views: **Outline**, the whole book as one
  tree in *reading* order (your `chapters:` list, not alphabetical), with the chapter you
  are editing open and the rest one row each, and any page the book never named gathered
  under **Unlisted** rather than quietly dropped; **References**,
  every cross-reference target with what points at it, with dangling ones grouped
  separately and open by default so a broken link is visible without running anything; and
  **Figures & Tables**, the numbered-float index. The two nesting views carry VS Code's
  **Collapse All** button. Clicking a row navigates. Nothing in the sidebar edits, and
  nothing reorders: the `.tmd` is the only editing surface.
- **Explorer badges** mark each `.tmd` with the worst problem `taliesin check` found in it,
  refreshed when you save. This is the one thing the squiggles cannot do for you: the
  language server only sees files you have opened, so a chapter you have not touched all
  week is invisible to it. Turn them off with `taliesin.explorerBadges` if you would rather
  not have `check` run on save.
- **The status bar** shows whether a preview is running for this project and on which port,
  plus the project's problem count. Click it to open or focus the preview.

### Build and check without leaving the editor

`Run Task` (`Ctrl+Shift+P`, "Tasks: Run Task") offers **check**, **build** and **build
--out** for the project you are in. Their output is parsed, so a `check` run fills the
**Problems** panel with real, clickable locations for **every page**, including the ones
you have never opened. A `suggestion` lands as information rather than as an error, because
advice is not a defect.

::: {.callout-note}
## Taliesin's tools are available to AI in the editor

The extension registers Taliesin's read-only commands as VS Code language-model tools, so a
model working in your editor can run `check`, read what a page actually renders to, list a
document's anchors, outline the project, or look up the accepted vocabulary. Reference one
in a prompt with `#tali-check`, `#tali-read`, `#tali-symbols`, `#tali-map` or `#tali-vocab`.

`build` is deliberately **not** offered: it writes files and executes your code cells, which
is not something to do on a model's hunch. The extension also advertises the `taliesin mcp`
server to VS Code, so an agent you point at it explicitly gets the wider set.
:::

## The dev menu

Every live preview carries a small **dev menu**: a `</>` button tucked in the
bottom corner of the page, styled after the Next.js dev indicator. It belongs to
the preview only, so a static `build` never ships it.

The button itself is a **connection light**: a coloured dot that reads *live* while
the websocket is connected, *reconnecting* while the server restarts, and *error*
on a failed render. A small badge appears on it when there are diagnostics waiting.

Click it to expand a panel with the preview-only tools:

- **Status** and a live **word count / reading time** for the prose.
- **Restart kernel**: drops the warm Jupyter kernel and re-runs every cell against
  a fresh one. Reach for it when the kernel wedges, or after you fix
  `TALIESIN_PYTHON` or install a missing package and want a clean replay.
- A reminder that **Ctrl-click (Cmd-click on Mac)** opens any block's source in
  your editor.
- A **theme toggle** (light/dark), but only when the page has no theme toggle of its
  own. A site puts one in its navbar, so the dev menu adds one only for a document
  previewed outside any project, whose chrome has none.
- The **diagnostics** list (detailed below), each entry click-to-source, with the
  button's badge tracking the count.
- **Changes**: what this session's edits actually did (below).
- **Sections**: the shape of the document, with what is wrong inside each one (below).

### Changes: what that edit actually did

Under **Changes** the panel keeps a running `+13 / −2 words` for the session and a
feed of your last dozen edits, newest first. Each row names the block and what
happened to it, and clicking a row opens that block's source:

```
± A probability table over 10 binary variab…  +4 −2
+ A freshly typed opening paragraph…          +9 −0
⇕ 16 blocks shifted
```

`±` is a block that changed, `+` one that appeared, `−` one that went away, and `⇕`
blocks whose line numbers moved because an edit above them changed the length of the
document. That last one is not busywork being reported: those blocks were *not*
re-rendered, so anything live inside them (a playing video, a `{js}` widget, an open
`<details>`) kept running while its source position was corrected underneath it.

The word figures are a real comparison of the words in and out, so rewording a
sentence reads `+4 −2` rather than `+2`. The two session totals count every edit
that way, which means their **difference** is exactly how much the document grew or
shrank while you worked, however you got there. The feed starts empty on every
reload: it is a record of this sitting, not a history of the file, which is what
version control is for.

### Sections: the shape of the draft

Below **Changes**, every heading in the page is listed at its own level, with the
weight of its section and any problem inside it. Clicking a row scrolls the preview
to that heading:

```
The Constraint That Quietly Breaks Your Optimizer   ⚠1 · TODO · 150w
Amplitudes: Turning the Constraint Into a Free Lunch        148w
  A nested subsection with a handful of words in it         138w
```

A section is the heading **plus everything down to the next heading of the same or
shallower level**, which is the same definition the outline and Move Section Down
use, so the three never disagree about where a section ends. That does mean a chapter
counts its own subsections: the numbers are for judging one section's weight, not for
adding up.

The badges are the same findings the diagnostics list shows, placed where they
happened: `✗` errors and `⚠` warnings whose source line falls inside that section, so
"which part of this draft is broken" is answerable without reading the list and
counting lines yourself. `TODO` counts your own `TODO`/`FIXME` markers in the prose,
which nothing in Taliesin validates and nothing will — they are notes to yourself, and
this only tells you where you left them.

This is a **revision** view, not the reader's table of contents. It is why it lives in
the dev menu, why its counts are about your draft rather than about the finished page,
and why a `build` ships none of it.

## Live-edit feedback

The loop surfaces what is happening without you watching the terminal:

- A non-fatal problem (a broken `{{< include >}}`, a missing kernel, a typo'd
  extension, a **broken `[@citation]` or `@cross-reference`**) appears in the dev
  menu's **diagnostics** list.
- A **broken front matter** points right at the bad line: the diagnostic shows a
  small code frame and is clickable to open that line in your editor.
- An advisory **accessibility audit** scans the rendered output for a handful of
  high-confidence, easy-to-miss issues (an image with no `alt` text, a heading
  level that skips, a link with no accessible name, a missing `<html lang>`, body
  text below the WCAG contrast floor) and lists them in the same panel, each
  click-to-source like any other diagnostic.
- A *failed* render throws a prominent, dismissible **error overlay** over the
  last good content, so a broken save is impossible to miss. It clears on the
  next successful render.
- The watcher follows the whole document, not just the served file: editing an
  included subsection, the `.bib`, or **theme CSS** refreshes the preview too, and
  a theme/CSS edit **hot-swaps in place** (no reload), so your scroll position
  and the current slide survive.

## Reading on any screen

The reading layout is responsive, so the same document works on a laptop and a
phone. Wide tables, long code, display math, and embedded canvases scroll within
their own box instead of stretching the page; type and spacing tighten on narrow
screens; in-page links scroll smoothly; and everything honours
`prefers-reduced-motion`.

On a phone the table of contents becomes a **pull-up sheet**: a labelled
**Contents** button at the bottom edge that you press to open and press again to
close (or drag up, if you would rather), with the current section flashing in
above it as you scroll. It stays put while the sheet is open, so the way in is
also the way out. It is keyboard- and screen-reader-friendly (Enter/Space to
toggle, Escape to close).

To see it on a real device, start the server with `--host`:

```sh
cargo run -p taliesin-server -- preview docs/ --host
```

That binds your LAN, prints a `network` URL, and renders a **QR code** in the
terminal. Scan it and the *live* preview opens on your phone, updating as you edit
on your laptop.

Because `--host` exposes the preview to everyone on the network, non-loopback
access is gated by a **per-session access token** baked into that printed URL and
QR (`?t=…`); the first load sets a cookie so the rest of the session needs nothing
further. Loopback (`127.0.0.1`, your own machine) is exempt and never needs the
token. The token is regenerated every server start, so scan the current QR or copy
the current URL: an old link from a previous run stops working, and a bare
`http://<lan-ip>:<port>/` typed by hand (or by a snooping neighbour) is refused.

Pressing <kbd>Cmd/Ctrl-P</kbd> gives a clean print/PDF: the dev menu, the TOC
handle, and any diagnostics are hidden, the palette forces light, and content flows
full width without clipping code, tables, or math.
